AZURE REGIONS, AVAILABILITY ZONES & STORAGE REDUNDANCY
========================================

1. DATA CENTERS
----------------------------------------
A Data Center is a physical facility containing servers, networking
equipment, power, cooling, and other infrastructure used to run cloud
services.

Let's say 1 data center is down due to a particular circumstance, like
a natural disaster:


2. AVAILABILITY ZONE (AZ)
----------------------------------------
An Availability Zone is a physically separate location within an Azure
Region, designed with independent power, cooling, and networking.

- One Region can have multiple Availability Zones.
- If one Availability Zone fails, workloads in another zone can
  continue running.
- Example: Region A -> Zone A, Zone B, Zone C.

Missing point: Not every Azure Region supports Availability Zones.
Only certain regions (mostly larger/newer ones) are "AZ-enabled" -
some regions only have a single datacenter setup with no zones.


3. REGION
----------------------------------------
An Azure Region is a geographical area containing one or more
Availability Zones and Azure data centers.

- Example: Region A -> Zone A + Zone B + Zone C.
- Different regions are geographically separated.


4. REGION PAIRS
----------------------------------------
Azure pairs certain regions within the same geography to improve
disaster recovery and business continuity.

- Region A <-> Region B
- If a major disaster affects one region, services/data can
  potentially be recovered from the paired region.
- Region pairs provide protection at a regional level, beyond what
  Availability Zones provide.

Correction/update: Not all Azure regions are paired anymore. Region
pairing was Azure's original model, but many newer regions are
launched WITHOUT a designated pair (they rely on Availability Zones
within the region instead). So "region pairs" apply to older/certain
regions, not universally to every region today.


AVAILABILITY - WHY UP TO 99.99%
----------------------------------------
Layered protection: Multiple Data Centers -> Availability Zone ->
Multiple Zones -> Region -> Region Pair

- Data Center failure -> Other infrastructure can continue
- Availability Zone failure -> Other zones can continue
- Region failure -> Paired region can provide disaster-recovery
  capability

Note: the 99.99% figure specifically refers to the SLA achievable when
a resource (e.g. a Virtual Machine) is deployed across 2 or more
Availability Zones. A single VM on premium storage typically gets
99.9%; using Availability Zones raises that to 99.99%. It is not a
number that applies automatically to every Azure service.


----------------------------------------------
REPLICATION SETTINGS (CALLED "POLICIES" HERE)
----------------------------------------------
These work like insurance for the business - a customer only pays for
the level of protection they choose:

1. If the user wants their data/system protected up to zone level, so
   Azure only replicates data up to zone level.
2. If the user wants their data/system protected up to region level,
   so Azure replicates data up to region level.

The user pays according to the level of protection ("policy")
selected.

Terminology note: Azure does not officially call this a "policy" - the
official term is "redundancy option" or "replication setting" (chosen
when creating a storage account: LRS/ZRS/GRS/GZRS). The concept you've
described is correct, just worth knowing the official name for exam
purposes.


------
AZURE STORAGE REDUNDANCY OPTIONS
------
Azure provides different storage redundancy options to maintain
multiple copies of data and protect it from hardware, data-center,
zone, or regional failures.

--
1. LRS - Locally Redundant Storage

Three copies of data are maintained within a single physical
location/data center in the primary region. It is the lowest-cost
redundancy option and protects mainly against local hardware/storage
failures.

1 Data Center -> 3 Copies: replication of the data within the same
data center/location.

--
2. ZRS - Zone Redundant Storage

3 copies of data are synchronously replicated across multiple
Availability Zones within the same Azure Region. If one zone becomes
unavailable, the data remains available from another zone.

Simple: 1 Region -> 3 Availability Zones -> 1 Copy in each Zone. A
zone consists of one or more physically separate data centers with
independent power, cooling, and networking.

--
3. GRS - Geo-Redundant Storage

Data is stored using LRS in the primary region and asynchronously
replicated to another geographically separated secondary region. This
protects against a regional failure.

Simple:
Primary Region -> LRS -> 3 Copies
    -> Asynchronous Geo-Replication
Secondary Region -> LRS -> 3 Copies

The secondary copy is not directly readable by the user. Read access
to the secondary is available with RA-GRS (Read-Access GRS).

Missing point: because replication to the secondary region is
ASYNCHRONOUS, there is a small possible data-loss window if the
primary region fails before the latest writes are copied over (this
is measured as RPO - Recovery Point Objective, typically minutes).
Also, failing over to the secondary region with plain GRS is a
Microsoft-managed process during a major outage, or can be a
customer-initiated account failover.

--
4. GZRS - Geo-Zone-Redundant Storage

Data is synchronously replicated across Availability Zones in the
primary region and asynchronously replicated to a secondary region. It
combines zone-level protection + regional protection.

Simple:
Primary Region -> ZRS -> Multiple Availability Zones
    -> Asynchronous Geo-Replication
Secondary Region -> LRS -> 3 Copies

GZRS provides the highest level of redundancy among these four
options, but generally at a higher cost. Read access to the secondary
region requires RA-GZRS.

--
THE EASIEST WAY TO REMEMBER:

LRS: Same location
ZRS: Different zones, same region
GRS: Different regions
GZRS: Different zones + different region

Or:

LRS -> Local protection
ZRS -> Zone protection
GRS -> Regional protection
GZRS -> Zone + Regional protection

COST / PROTECTION IDEA

LRS -> Cheapest -> Least redundancy
ZRS -> More expensive -> Zone failure protection
GRS -> More expensive -> Regional failure protection
GZRS -> Highest among these -> Zone + regional failure protection
